At least now I know what to do, but I stumbled a lot in the process.  From a design philosophy perspective, I see data.table as designed around specific ways of doing things (e.g. assuming that keys are set for joins, assuming that the first columns of Y match the key of X or that Y has a key, etc.).  If your needs don't match these design assumptions, then you have to make modifications.  An alternative approach is to write the syntax for the general case, but implement important optimizations.  For example, always perform a natural join (matching corresponding names in X and Y) for any X[Y], add something like X[Y, by=(Xcol1=Ycol1, ...)] and if the matching uses the key for X, then all the better. 

